{"name":"Back to Basics","hex":"#726747","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"back to basics","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["back to fundamental principles"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A conscious return to principles such as self-respect, decency and honesty; especially the discredited policy of the Conservative government in the 1990s."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/back_to_basics"}],"entries":[{"title":"Back to Basics (The Temptations album)","blocks":[{"text":"Back to Basics is the second of two studio albums released by American soul group The Temptations in 1983. The album saw the group reunited with former producer Norman Whitfield, and would also be the first to feature Ron Tyson as first tenor, who would eventually become the second-longest serving member in the group’s history. It would also be the last studio album with longtime frontman Dennis Edwards before his second departure and the first appearance of Edwards’ successor Ali-Ollie Woodson."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/d/d0/The_Temptations_-_Back_to_Basics.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Back_to_Basics_(The_Temptations_album)","wikidata":"Q118175547"},{"title":"Back to Basics (Billy Bragg album)","blocks":[{"text":"Back to Basics is a 1987 collection of Billy Bragg's first three releases: The albums Life's a Riot with Spy Vs. Spy and Brewing Up with Billy Bragg, and the EP Between the Wars—all of which make their debut in the United States here. This collection did not contain any new material, but did document Billy Bragg's early \"one man and his guitar\" approach. The songs collected on this release demonstrate major recurrent themes in Bragg's work: highly critical commentary on Thatcherite Britain, laced with poetic love songs. The collection was re-released in November 1993 on the Cooking Vinyl label."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/e/ec/BillyBraggBacktoBasics.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Back_to_Basics_(Billy_Bragg_album)","wikidata":"Q4839384"},{"title":"Back to Basics (Christina Aguilera album)","blocks":[{"text":"Back to Basics is the fifth studio album by American singer Christina Aguilera. It was released on August 9, 2006, in the United States by RCA Records as a double album. Serving as executive producer, she enlisted a wide range of producers, including DJ Premier, Rich Harrison, Rob Lewis, Mark Ronson, and Linda Perry. Recording sessions took place between February 2005 and April 2006 at several studios in the United States and the United Kingdom."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/4/47/Christina_Aguilera_-_Back_to_Basics.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Back_to_Basics_(Christina_Aguilera_album)","wikidata":"Q646508"},{"title":"Human back","blocks":[{"text":"The human back, also called the dorsum, is the large posterior area of the human body, rising from the top of the buttocks to the back of the neck. It is the surface of the body opposite from the chest and the abdomen. The vertebral column runs the length of the back and creates a central area of recession. The breadth of the back is created by the shoulders at the top and the pelvis at the bottom."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Posterior_view_of_human_female_and_male_backs_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Posterior_view_of_human_female_and_male_backs_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human_back","wikidata":"Q133279"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Candyman_Sweet_Sugar.jpg/500px-Candyman_Sweet_Sugar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Candyman_Sweet_Sugar.jpg/500px-Candyman_Sweet_Sugar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Aguilera performing \"Candyman\", the album's third single, on the Back to Basics Tour (2006–2008)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACandyman_Sweet_Sugar.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Christina_Aguilera_-_Back_to_Basics_Tour_-_Ain%27t_No_Other_Man.jpg/500px-Christina_Aguilera_-_Back_to_Basics_Tour_-_Ain%27t_No_Other_Man.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Christina_Aguilera_-_Back_to_Basics_Tour_-_Ain%27t_No_Other_Man.jpg/500px-Christina_Aguilera_-_Back_to_Basics_Tour_-_Ain%27t_No_Other_Man.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Aguilera performing \"Ain't No Other Man\" during the Back to Basics Tour; the song peaked at number six on the US Billboard Hot 100, and reached the top five of record charts of several countries","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChristina_Aguilera_-_Back_to_Basics_Tour_-_Ain't_No_Other_Man.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Gray204.png/120px-Gray204.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Gray204.png/250px-Gray204.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Gray204.png/120px-Gray204.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGray204.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Back_Muscles.jpg/120px-Back_Muscles.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=pars